                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 862,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1266,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Finds the peaks of a curve - the points higher than their neighbours - and gives their positions, their heights and how many there are. With valleys chosen it finds the dips instead.\n\nEvery little wiggle is a peak, so the useful work is in choosing which peaks count. Prominence is the best tool: how far a peak stands above the surrounding ground - a small ripple on a big wave has little prominence however high it is. Spacing keeps only the highest of peaks closer together than a minimum; a minimum height ignores everything below it.\n\nUse it on a recorded curve to find footfalls in a vertical position, beats in an envelope, the accents of a gesture in a speed, the turning points of a joint angle, the onsets of a movement.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.find_peaks <prominence: float> <spacing: int>\nDefaults: prominence 0 (off), spacing 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nscipy.find_peaks 0.5 10\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe curve: a list or 1-D array. Every input is searched and sent. A nan is treated as a deep gap, so it does not hide the peaks beside it.\n\nprominence (float box, also an inlet):\nHow far a peak must stand above the ground around it. 0 counts every peak.\n\nspacing (int box, also an inlet):\nThe smallest distance between peaks, in samples. Where two are closer, only the higher is kept.\n\nmin height (float box, also an inlet):\nIgnore peaks below this - for valleys, dips above it. Used only when use min height is on.\n\nuse min height:\nAn option, under the node: turns the minimum height on.\n\nfind:\nAn option, under the node: peaks (the default) or valleys.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nindices:\nThe position of each peak, in samples from the start.\n\nheights:\nThe value of the curve at each peak.\n\ncount:\nHow many peaks. Sent first, then the heights, then the indices.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe wobble comes from the make_wobble subpatcher (double-click it to look inside):\n120 samples - two seconds at 60 a second - of a slow wave going round 3 times, with a ripple five times as fast and a third as tall added to it.\n\nWith prominence 0, every crest of the ripple counts: 15 peaks. With prominence 1, only the three crests of the slow wave stand out far enough: at samples 10, 50 and 90, each 1.3 high (the wave's 1 plus the ripple's 0.3 riding on top).\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.filtfilt smooths a curve first, so that its peaks are the shape's and not the noise's. np.argmax finds the single highest point. np.diff and np.sign show where a curve turns.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1250,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Finds the peaks of a curve - the points higher than their neighbours - and gives their positions, their heights and how many there are. With valleys chosen it finds the dips instead.\n\nEvery little wiggle is a peak, so the useful work is in choosing which peaks count. Prominence is the best tool: how far a peak stands above the surrounding ground - a small ripple on a big wave has little prominence however high it is. Spacing keeps only the highest of peaks closer together than a minimum; a minimum height ignores everything below it.\n\nUse it on a recorded curve to find footfalls in a vertical position, beats in an envelope, the accents of a gesture in a speed, the turning points of a joint angle, the onsets of a movement.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.find_peaks <prominence: float> <spacing: int>\nDefaults: prominence 0 (off), spacing 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nscipy.find_peaks 0.5 10\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe curve: a list or 1-D array. Every input is searched and sent. A nan is treated as a deep gap, so it does not hide the peaks beside it.\n\nprominence (float box, also an inlet):\nHow far a peak must stand above the ground around it. 0 counts every peak.\n\nspacing (int box, also an inlet):\nThe smallest distance between peaks, in samples. Where two are closer, only the higher is kept.\n\nmin height (float box, also an inlet):\nIgnore peaks below this - for valleys, dips above it. Used only when use min height is on.\n\nuse min height:\nAn option, under the node: turns the minimum height on.\n\nfind:\nAn option, under the node: peaks (the default) or valleys.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nindices:\nThe position of each peak, in samples from the start.\n\nheights:\nThe value of the curve at each peak.\n\ncount:\nHow many peaks. Sent first, then the heights, then the indices.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe wobble comes from the make_wobble subpatcher (double-click it to look inside):\n120 samples - two seconds at 60 a second - of a slow wave going round 3 times, with a ripple five times as fast and a third as tall added to it.\n\nWith prominence 0, every crest of the ripple counts: 15 peaks. With prominence 1, only the three crests of the slow wave stand out far enough: at samples 10, 50 and 90, each 1.3 high (the wave's 1 plus the ripple's 0.3 riding on top).\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.filtfilt smooths a curve first, so that its peaks are the shape's and not the noise's. np.argmax finds the single highest point. np.diff and np.sign show where a curve turns."
                },
